Trading Dashboard
Version: V2 — Updated 30-Aug-2026
1. Purpose
This document defines the final V2 performance and application architecture for the Trading Dashboard.

The objective is to improve startup performance, refresh performance, memory usage, chart synchronization, and scalability without repeatedly redesigning the application architecture.

V2 must establish clear ownership between:

Workbook/data loading
Contract data management
Timeframe transformation
Chart data preparation
JavaScript/browser rendering
Crosshair synchronization
Layout management
Performance monitoring
The architecture defined here should be treated as the baseline architecture for all subsequent development.

2. Confirmed Data Model
The application uses a workbook containing multiple contract sheets.

Example:

Workbook.xlsx
│
├── NIFTY
├── SENSEX
├── BANKNIFTY
├── NIFTY2681124750
├── NIFTY2681124800
├── ...
└── many other contract sheets

Each worksheet represents an independently selectable contract/instrument.

A chart can select any available contract.

Multiple charts may select the same contract.

Multiple charts may also select the same contract with different timeframes.

Example:

Chart 1 → NIFTY → 1m
Chart 2 → NIFTY → 1m
Chart 3 → NIFTY → 5m
Chart 4 → BANKNIFTY → 1m
Chart 5 → BANKNIFTY → 5m
Chart 6 → SENSEX → 1m
Chart 7 → NIFTY2681124750 → 1m
Chart 8 → NIFTY2681124750 → 1m

This requirement is fundamental to the V2 architecture.

3. Core V2 Architecture Principle
The application must use shared data ownership.

A chart must not independently load and process the same workbook sheet every time it refreshes.

The central principle is:

One contract
      ↓
One raw-data load
      ↓
Shared raw data
      ↓
Timeframe-specific processing
      ↓
Shared processed data
      ↓
Multiple charts

Therefore:

The cache/data identity is based on (contract, timeframe), while raw data is cached by contract.

