5. Responsibility Separation
V2 must maintain strict separation between the following layers.

5.1 Data Source Layer
Responsible for:

Reading workbook sheets
Reading raw contract data
Handling Excel-specific operations
Reporting workbook/file errors
This layer must not know about charts.

Existing excel_loader.py belongs here.

5.2 Data Repository Layer
A new central repository layer must own application data.

Responsibilities:

Raw contract caching
Timeframe caching
Lazy loading
Cache lookup
Cache invalidation
Workbook modification detection
Preventing duplicate loads
Providing data to multiple charts
The repository must be the single data access point for chart consumers.

Conceptually:

data = repository.get(
    contract="NIFTY",
    timeframe="1m",
)

The chart must not call:

load_sheet(...)

directly.

5.3 Raw Contract Cache
Raw workbook data must be cached using:

contract

as the primary key.

Example:

raw_cache:

"NIFTY"              → DataFrame
"SENSEX"             → DataFrame
"BANKNIFTY"          → DataFrame
"NIFTY2681124750"    → DataFrame

If four charts use NIFTY:

Chart 1 ─┐
Chart 2 ─┤
Chart 3 ─┼──→ raw_cache["NIFTY"]
Chart 4 ─┘

Only one Excel read should be required while the cache is valid.

6. Timeframe Cache
Processed timeframe data must be cached using:

(contract, timeframe)

as the cache key.

Examples:

("NIFTY", "1m")
("NIFTY", "5m")
("NIFTY", "15m")
("BANKNIFTY", "1m")
("SENSEX", "1D")

Example:

NIFTY raw data
      │
      ├── 1m  ──→ cached
      ├── 5m  ──→ cached
      ├── 15m ──→ cached
      └── 1D  ──→ cached

Multiple charts using the same contract/timeframe must reuse the same processed dataset.

7. Lazy Loading Requirement
The repository must use lazy loading.

The application must NOT load every workbook sheet during startup.

Example:

Workbook
   │
   ├── NIFTY
   ├── SENSEX
   ├── BANKNIFTY
   ├── 500 other contracts
   │
   ▼
Application starts
   │
   ▼
Load only contracts currently required

If the user only displays:

NIFTY
SENSEX
BANKNIFTY

only those contracts should be loaded.

8. Duplicate Load Prevention
The repository must guarantee that simultaneous or sequential requests for the same contract do not unnecessarily cause repeated workbook reads.

Example:

Chart 1 → NIFTY
Chart 2 → NIFTY
Chart 3 → NIFTY
Chart 4 → NIFTY

Expected:

Excel:
    NIFTY → 1 load

Repository:
    NIFTY → shared

Charts:
    4 consumers

Not:

NIFTY → 4 Excel loads

9. Workbook Change Detection
The workbook is externally changeable.

V2 must detect workbook changes.

At minimum, the repository must track:

Workbook path
Workbook modification timestamp

Conceptually:

Workbook unchanged
      ↓
reuse cache

Workbook changed
      ↓
invalidate affected cache
      ↓
reload on next request

The system must not reload Excel merely because a chart refreshes.

10. Cache Invalidation
When the workbook changes:

Raw contract cache
        ↓
invalidate
        ↓
Timeframe cache
        ↓
invalidate

At minimum, all cached derived data associated with changed raw data must become invalid.

The architecture should permit future optimization toward sheet-level invalidation if workbook change tracking can identify the affected sheet.

11. ChartSlot Responsibility
ChartSlot must become primarily a view/controller consumer.

It should own:

Selected contract
Selected timeframe
Chart/browser instance
Chart display state
User interaction state
It should request data from the repository.

It should NOT own:

Excel loading
Global data caching
Duplicate prevention
Workbook change detection
Shared DataFrame ownership
Target flow:

ChartSlot
   │
   │ request contract + timeframe
   ▼
DataRepository
   │
   ▼
Chart-ready data
   │
   ▼
ChartSlot
   │
   ▼
JavaScript chart

12. Refresh Pipeline
The V2 refresh pipeline should become:

Chart refresh requested
        │
        ▼
Identify contract
        │
        ▼
Identify timeframe
        │
        ▼
DataRepository.get(contract, timeframe)
        │
        ├── raw cache hit
        │
        └── raw cache miss → load workbook sheet
        │
        ▼
Timeframe cache lookup
        │
        ├── hit → reuse
        │
        └── miss → transform raw data
        │
        ▼
Prepare chart payload
        │
        ▼
Serialize payload
        │
        ▼
Submit JavaScript
        │
        ▼
Browser renders chart

13. Chart Payload Layer
The repository should eventually return a well-defined chart-data representation rather than exposing internal DataFrame implementation details to the UI.

Conceptually:

DataFrame
    ↓
ChartData
    ↓
ChartSlot
    ↓
JavaScript

This creates an architectural boundary that allows future replacement of:

Pandas
Excel
JSON format
chart library
data source
without redesigning the entire application.

14. Timeframe Processing
Timeframe transformation must be performed once per:

(contract, timeframe)

rather than once per chart.

Example:

NIFTY / 5m

used by five charts must be processed once.

The five charts consume the same processed result.

15. 1-Minute Data
For 1m timeframe, the repository should avoid unnecessary transformations.

If the source data is already 1-minute data:

raw 1m
   ↓
reuse / normalize only when required

Avoid unnecessary:

DataFrame copies
sorting
conversions
aggregation
when data is already normalized and valid.

This optimization should be implemented only after preserving correctness.

16. Higher Timeframes
For higher timeframes:

1m → 5m
1m → 15m
1m → 30m
1m → 1h
1m → 1D

aggregation should happen from the shared raw contract data.

Example:

NIFTY raw 1m
      │
      ├── 5m
      ├── 15m
      ├── 30m
      └── 1D

Each result is cached.

17. Crosshair Synchronization
Crosshair synchronization is a separate performance domain.

It must NOT be mixed with data-loading optimization.

Current behavior can involve multiple:

runJavaScript(...)

calls across charts.

V2 should establish a dedicated:

CrosshairController

or equivalent centralized synchronization mechanism.

Target:

User moves crosshair
        │
        ▼
Identify source chart
        │
        ▼
Calculate synchronization coordinate
        │
        ▼
Notify eligible charts
        │
        ▼
Update crosshairs

The system should avoid unnecessary updates to:

hidden charts
source chart
charts that do not need updating
charts already at the requested crosshair position
18. Crosshair Synchronization Scope
Crosshair synchronization must support:

Main dashboard charts
Popup charts where applicable
Multiple contracts
Different timeframes
The synchronization mechanism must not assume that all charts contain identical timestamps.

For different contracts/timeframes, timestamp matching must be handled explicitly.

Example:

NIFTY / 1m
SENSEX / 5m
BANKNIFTY / 15m

The crosshair system must determine the appropriate corresponding timestamp/index rather than assuming identical candle positions.

19. JavaScript Communication
The application currently has multiple runJavaScript() call sites.

V2 should classify them into categories:

1. Chart data
2. Crosshair
3. Drawing
4. Chart initialization
5. UI state
6. Popup state
7. Resize/layout
8. Other UI commands

Only chart-data communication belongs to the initial data-performance pipeline.

Other JavaScript communication must be optimized independently.

20. Chart Data Transfer Optimization
The current measured chart payload is approximately:

34,490 bytes

for 375 candles.

Current measured serialization:

~0.4–0.8 ms

and JavaScript submission:

~0.2–0.4 ms

Therefore JSON serialization and Python-side JavaScript submission are not currently the primary bottleneck.

Optimization must not be performed prematurely.

Future optimization may consider:

incremental updates
binary formats
compact JSON
reduced fields
browser-side data retention
but only if measurements demonstrate a real bottleneck.

